Below is a minimal integration test that boots the full application context, sends an HTTP request, and asserts on the response:

```java
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = SpringBootTest.WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)
class OrderControllerIT {

    @Autowired
    private TestRestTemplate restTemplate; // auto-configured by Spring Boot

    @Autowired
    private OrderRepository orderRepository;

    @BeforeEach
    void setUp() {
        orderRepository.deleteAll(); // clean state for each test
    }

    @Test
    void createOrder_shouldPersistAndReturnCreated() {
        OrderRequest request = new OrderRequest("Widget", 3);

        ResponseEntity<OrderResponse> response = restTemplate
                .postForEntity("/api/orders", request, OrderResponse.class);

        assertThat(response.getStatusCode()).isEqualTo(HttpStatus.CREATED);
        assertThat(orderRepository.count()).isEqualTo(1); // verify DB side-effect
    }
}
```

Notice that no mocks are used. The test exercises the full stack: HTTP serialization → controller → service → repository → H2 database. If any layer is misconfigured the test fails, giving high confidence that the feature works end-to-end.

The most common pattern for full-stack integration tests uses `RANDOM_PORT` so tests don't collide with running applications:

```java
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = SpringBootTest.WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)
class UserControllerIT {

    @Autowired
    private TestRestTemplate restTemplate; // bound to the random port automatically

    @LocalServerPort
    private int port; // injected for logging or custom URLs

    @Test
    void getUser_shouldReturnOk() {
        ResponseEntity<UserDto> resp = restTemplate
                .getForEntity("/api/users/1", UserDto.class);

        assertThat(resp.getStatusCode()).isEqualTo(HttpStatus.OK);
        assertThat(resp.getBody().getName()).isEqualTo("Alice");
    }
}
```

Spring Boot auto-configures `TestRestTemplate` when `RANDOM_PORT` is active. It already knows the base URL, so you only need to supply the path.

Here is a complete example using `@Testcontainers` with JUnit 5 and Spring Boot:

```java
@SpringBootTest
@Testcontainers
class OrderRepositoryIT {

    @Container // manages lifecycle: start before tests, stop after
    static PostgreSQLContainer<?> postgres =
            new PostgreSQLContainer<>("postgres:15-alpine")
                    .withDatabaseName("testdb")
                    .withUsername("test")
                    .withPassword("test");

    @DynamicPropertySource // injects container connection details into Spring
    static void configureProperties(DynamicPropertyRegistry registry) {
        registry.add("spring.datasource.url", postgres::getJdbcUrl);
        registry.add("spring.datasource.username", postgres::getUsername);
        registry.add("spring.datasource.password", postgres::getPassword);
    }

    @Autowired
    private OrderRepository orderRepository;

    @Test
    void save_shouldPersistOrder() {
        Order order = new Order("Widget", 5, BigDecimal.valueOf(9.99));
        Order saved = orderRepository.save(order);

        assertThat(saved.getId()).isNotNull();
        assertThat(orderRepository.findById(saved.getId())).isPresent();
    }
}
```

The `@Container` annotation tells JUnit to start/stop the container. `@DynamicPropertySource` feeds the random JDBC URL into Spring's `DataSource` configuration so Hibernate connects to the containerized Postgres.

Starting a new container per test class is reliable but slow (~2–5 seconds per container start). The **singleton container pattern** starts the container once and reuses it across all test classes:

```java
public abstract class AbstractIntegrationTest {

    static final PostgreSQLContainer<?> POSTGRES;

    static {
        POSTGRES = new PostgreSQLContainer<>("postgres:15-alpine");
        POSTGRES.start(); // started once, JVM shutdown hook cleans up
    }

    @DynamicPropertySource
    static void configure(DynamicPropertyRegistry registry) {
        registry.add("spring.datasource.url", POSTGRES::getJdbcUrl);
        registry.add("spring.datasource.username", POSTGRES::getUsername);
        registry.add("spring.datasource.password", POSTGRES::getPassword);
    }
}
```

All integration test classes extend `AbstractIntegrationTest`. The container starts once in the static initializer and is reused until the JVM exits. Combined with Spring context caching, this approach keeps suite runtime manageable even with dozens of integration tests.

The trade-off is test isolation: since all tests share the same database, you need `@Transactional` rollback or explicit cleanup (`deleteAll()` in `@BeforeEach`) to prevent data leakage between tests.

Here is a typical `@WebMvcTest` for a `ProductController`:

```java
@WebMvcTest(ProductController.class) // loads only this controller
class ProductControllerTest {

    @Autowired
    private MockMvc mockMvc; // auto-configured by the slice

    @MockBean
    private ProductService productService; // mock replaces real bean

    @Test
    void getProduct_shouldReturnJson() throws Exception {
        ProductDto dto = new ProductDto(1L, "Laptop", BigDecimal.valueOf(999));
        when(productService.findById(1L)).thenReturn(dto);

        mockMvc.perform(get("/api/products/1")
                        .accept(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON))
                .andExpect(status().isOk())
                .andExpect(jsonPath("$.name").value("Laptop"))
                .andExpect(jsonPath("$.price").value(999));
    }

    @Test
    void createProduct_withInvalidName_shouldReturn400() throws Exception {
        String invalidBody = "{\"name\": \"\", \"price\": 10}";

        mockMvc.perform(post("/api/products")
                        .contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
                        .content(invalidBody))
                .andExpect(status().isBadRequest()); // @NotBlank triggers 400
    }
}
```

The first test stubs the service and verifies JSON serialization. The second test verifies that Bean Validation annotations on the request body work correctly. Neither test touches a database.

```java
@DataJpaTest
class ProductRepositoryTest {

    @Autowired
    private TestEntityManager em; // helper for persist/flush/find

    @Autowired
    private ProductRepository productRepository;

    @Test
    void findByCategory_shouldReturnMatchingProducts() {
        Product laptop = new Product("Laptop", "Electronics", BigDecimal.valueOf(999));
        Product phone = new Product("Phone", "Electronics", BigDecimal.valueOf(699));
        Product book = new Product("Clean Code", "Books", BigDecimal.valueOf(40));

        em.persist(laptop);
        em.persist(phone);
        em.persist(book);
        em.flush(); // force SQL execution so queries see the data

        List<Product> electronics = productRepository.findByCategory("Electronics");

        assertThat(electronics).hasSize(2)
                .extracting(Product::getName)
                .containsExactlyInAnyOrder("Laptop", "Phone");
    }
}
```

`TestEntityManager` is a testing-friendly wrapper around JPA's `EntityManager`. Using `persist` + `flush` guarantees data is in the database before the query runs. The `@Transactional` rollback means this data disappears after the test—no cleanup needed.

By default `@DataJpaTest` replaces your configured `DataSource` with an embedded H2. This is fast but may miss dialect-specific bugs. To test against the real database, add `@AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace = Replace.NONE)` and use Testcontainers:

```java
@DataJpaTest
@AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace = Replace.NONE)
@Testcontainers
class ProductRepositoryRealDbTest {

    @Container
    static PostgreSQLContainer<?> pg =
            new PostgreSQLContainer<>("postgres:15-alpine");

    @DynamicPropertySource
    static void props(DynamicPropertyRegistry r) {
        r.add("spring.datasource.url", pg::getJdbcUrl);
        r.add("spring.datasource.username", pg::getUsername);
        r.add("spring.datasource.password", pg::getPassword);
    }

    // ... same test methods, now running against real PostgreSQL
}
```

This approach is ideal for native queries that use Postgres-specific features like `jsonb`, `array_agg`, or window functions.

```java
@WebMvcTest(UserController.class)
class UserControllerTest {

    @Autowired
    private MockMvc mockMvc;

    @MockBean
    private UserService userService;

    @Test
    void getUser_shouldReturnJsonBody() throws Exception {
        when(userService.findById(1L))
                .thenReturn(new UserDto(1L, "Alice", "alice@example.com"));

        mockMvc.perform(get("/api/users/1")
                        .accept(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON))
                .andExpect(status().isOk())
                .andExpect(content().contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON))
                .andExpect(jsonPath("$.name").value("Alice"))
                .andExpect(jsonPath("$.email").value("alice@example.com"));
    }

    @Test
    void createUser_withBlankName_shouldReturn400() throws Exception {
        mockMvc.perform(post("/api/users")
                        .contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
                        .content("{\"name\": \"\", \"email\": \"a@b.com\"}"))
                .andExpect(status().isBadRequest())
                .andExpect(jsonPath("$.errors[0].field").value("name"));
    }

    @Test
    void deleteUser_shouldReturnNoContent() throws Exception {
        mockMvc.perform(delete("/api/users/1"))
                .andExpect(status().isNoContent());

        verify(userService).deleteById(1L); // confirm service was called
    }
}
```

Each test follows the pattern: set up mocks → perform request → assert status, headers, and body. The `jsonPath` assertions use JSONPath syntax to drill into nested JSON structures.

```java
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = SpringBootTest.WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)
class UserApiIT {

    @Autowired
    private TestRestTemplate restTemplate;

    @Autowired
    private UserRepository userRepository;

    @BeforeEach
    void cleanUp() {
        userRepository.deleteAll();
    }

    @Test
    void createAndRetrieveUser() {
        // POST: create a user
        UserRequest req = new UserRequest("Alice", "alice@mail.com");
        ResponseEntity<UserResponse> createResp =
                restTemplate.postForEntity("/api/users", req, UserResponse.class);

        assertThat(createResp.getStatusCode()).isEqualTo(HttpStatus.CREATED);
        Long userId = createResp.getBody().getId();

        // GET: retrieve the same user
        ResponseEntity<UserResponse> getResp =
                restTemplate.getForEntity("/api/users/" + userId, UserResponse.class);

        assertThat(getResp.getStatusCode()).isEqualTo(HttpStatus.OK);
        assertThat(getResp.getBody().getName()).isEqualTo("Alice");
    }

    @Test
    void getUser_notFound_shouldReturn404() {
        ResponseEntity<String> resp =
                restTemplate.getForEntity("/api/users/999", String.class);

        assertThat(resp.getStatusCode()).isEqualTo(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND);
    }
}
```

Note how `TestRestTemplate` does not throw on a 404—it returns the `ResponseEntity` with the status code for you to assert. This fault-tolerant behavior is intentional: tests should assert on status codes, not catch exceptions.

For secured endpoints, `TestRestTemplate` provides `withBasicAuth()`:

```java
restTemplate.withBasicAuth("admin", "secret")
        .getForEntity("/api/admin/dashboard", DashboardDto.class);
```

For Bearer tokens or custom headers, use `exchange()` with `HttpHeaders`:

```java
HttpHeaders headers = new HttpHeaders();
headers.setBearerAuth(jwtToken);
HttpEntity<Void> entity = new HttpEntity<>(headers);

ResponseEntity<UserResponse> resp = restTemplate.exchange(
        "/api/users/me", HttpMethod.GET, entity, UserResponse.class);
```

This flexibility makes `TestRestTemplate` suitable for testing secured APIs end-to-end, including JWT validation, role-based access control, and CSRF protection.

In `src/test/resources/application-test.properties`:

```properties
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:h2:mem:testdb;DB_CLOSE_DELAY=-1
spring.datasource.driver-class-name=org.h2.Driver
spring.datasource.username=sa
spring.datasource.password=
spring.jpa.database-platform=org.hibernate.dialect.H2Dialect
spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=create-drop
```

The `DB_CLOSE_DELAY=-1` flag keeps the database alive as long as the JVM runs, preventing premature shutdown between test methods. With `@DataJpaTest`, you usually don't even need this file—Spring Boot auto-configures everything.

For a complete test:

```java
@DataJpaTest
@ActiveProfiles("test")
class CustomerRepositoryTest {

    @Autowired
    private CustomerRepository repo;

    @Test
    void findByEmail_shouldReturnCustomer() {
        repo.save(new Customer("Alice", "alice@test.com"));

        Optional<Customer> found = repo.findByEmail("alice@test.com");

        assertThat(found).isPresent();
        assertThat(found.get().getName()).isEqualTo("Alice");
    }
}
```

H2 boots in ~100ms, the test runs, and `@Transactional` rolls back the insert automatically.

```java
@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class) // enables Mockito annotations
class OrderServiceTest {

    @Mock
    private OrderRepository orderRepository;

    @Mock
    private PaymentGateway paymentGateway;

    @InjectMocks // creates OrderService and injects the mocks above
    private OrderService orderService;

    @Test
    void placeOrder_shouldChargeAndPersist() {
        // Arrange: define mock behavior
        when(paymentGateway.charge(anyString(), any(BigDecimal.class)))
                .thenReturn(new PaymentResult(true, "txn-123"));
        when(orderRepository.save(any(Order.class)))
                .thenAnswer(inv -> {
                    Order o = inv.getArgument(0);
                    o.setId(1L);
                    return o;
                });

        // Act
        Order result = orderService.placeOrder("card-456", "Widget", BigDecimal.TEN);

        // Assert
        assertThat(result.getId()).isEqualTo(1L);
        assertThat(result.getTransactionId()).isEqualTo("txn-123");

        // Verify interactions
        verify(paymentGateway).charge("card-456", BigDecimal.TEN);
        verify(orderRepository).save(any(Order.class));
    }
}
```

This test runs in milliseconds with no database, no network, and no payment provider. It proves that `OrderService.placeOrder()` correctly coordinates between its two dependencies.

```java
@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class NotificationServiceTest {

    @Mock
    private EmailClient emailClient; // mock created before each test

    @Mock
    private UserRepository userRepository;

    @InjectMocks
    private NotificationService notificationService;

    @Test
    void notifyUser_shouldSendEmail() {
        User user = new User(1L, "Alice", "alice@test.com");
        when(userRepository.findById(1L)).thenReturn(Optional.of(user));
        when(emailClient.send(anyString(), anyString(), anyString())).thenReturn(true);

        boolean sent = notificationService.notifyUser(1L, "Welcome!");

        assertThat(sent).isTrue();
        verify(emailClient).send("alice@test.com", "Notification", "Welcome!");
    }

    @Test
    void notifyUser_whenUserNotFound_shouldReturnFalse() {
        when(userRepository.findById(99L)).thenReturn(Optional.empty());

        boolean sent = notificationService.notifyUser(99L, "Hello");

        assertThat(sent).isFalse();
        verify(emailClient, never()).send(anyString(), anyString(), anyString());
    }
}
```

Each test gets a fresh mock instance. The mock state (stubs, recorded invocations) does not leak between tests because `MockitoExtension` reinitializes the fields before each method.

```java
@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class PaymentServiceTest {

    @Mock
    private PaymentGateway paymentGateway;

    @Mock
    private TransactionRepository transactionRepo;

    @Mock
    private NotificationService notificationService;

    @InjectMocks // creates PaymentService with all three mocks injected
    private PaymentService paymentService;

    @Test
    void processPayment_success_shouldNotifyAndSave() {
        when(paymentGateway.charge("card-1", BigDecimal.TEN))
                .thenReturn(ChargeResult.success("txn-42"));

        PaymentResult result = paymentService.processPayment("card-1", BigDecimal.TEN);

        assertThat(result.isSuccessful()).isTrue();
        verify(transactionRepo).save(any(Transaction.class));
        verify(notificationService).sendReceipt(anyString());
    }
}
```

Mockito creates `PaymentService` by finding a constructor that accepts `PaymentGateway`, `TransactionRepository`, and `NotificationService`, then passes the three mocks. The test verifies real business logic inside `processPayment()` while all I/O is faked.

Understanding which injection strategy Mockito uses helps avoid surprises:

**Constructor injection (recommended)**:
```java
public class PaymentService {
    private final PaymentGateway gateway;
    private final TransactionRepository repo;

    public PaymentService(PaymentGateway gateway, TransactionRepository repo) {
        this.gateway = gateway;
        this.repo = repo;
    }
}
```
Mockito matches constructor parameters by type. If multiple constructors exist, it uses the one with the most parameters.

**Field injection (fallback)**:
```java
public class PaymentService {
    @Autowired private PaymentGateway gateway;
    @Autowired private TransactionRepository repo;
}
```
Mockito injects via reflection. This works but is fragile—if you add a new dependency and forget to add a `@Mock`, Mockito silently injects null. With constructor injection, you'd get a compilation error or a clear Mockito error.

Best practice: design your classes with **constructor injection**. It works with both Spring and Mockito, supports `final` fields, and makes missing dependencies obvious.

```java
@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class UserServiceTest {

    @Mock
    private UserRepository userRepository;

    @InjectMocks
    private UserService userService;

    @Test
    void findUser_shouldReturnUser() {
        User alice = new User(1L, "Alice");
        when(userRepository.findById(1L)).thenReturn(Optional.of(alice));

        User result = userService.findUser(1L);

        assertThat(result.getName()).isEqualTo("Alice");
    }

    @Test
    void findUser_notFound_shouldThrow() {
        when(userRepository.findById(99L)).thenReturn(Optional.empty());

        assertThatThrownBy(() -> userService.findUser(99L))
                .isInstanceOf(UserNotFoundException.class);
    }

    @Test
    void saveUser_shouldReturnSavedEntity() {
        // thenAnswer for dynamic responses based on input
        when(userRepository.save(any(User.class))).thenAnswer(invocation -> {
            User u = invocation.getArgument(0);
            u.setId(42L); // simulate auto-generated ID
            return u;
        });

        User saved = userService.createUser("Bob");

        assertThat(saved.getId()).isEqualTo(42L);
    }
}
```

The first two tests use `thenReturn()` with fixed values. The third uses `thenAnswer()` for dynamic behavior—the answer lambda receives the actual arguments and can compute a return value.

```java
@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class OrderServiceTest {

    @Mock private OrderRepository orderRepo;
    @Mock private EventPublisher eventPublisher;
    @Mock private AuditLogger auditLogger;

    @InjectMocks private OrderService orderService;

    @Test
    void cancelOrder_shouldDeleteAndPublishEvent() {
        Order order = new Order(1L, "Widget", OrderStatus.ACTIVE);
        when(orderRepo.findById(1L)).thenReturn(Optional.of(order));

        orderService.cancelOrder(1L);

        // Verify exact call with specific arguments
        verify(orderRepo).delete(order);

        // Verify with argument matcher
        verify(eventPublisher).publish(argThat(event ->
                event.getType().equals("ORDER_CANCELLED") &&
                event.getOrderId().equals(1L)));

        // Verify call count
        verify(auditLogger, times(1)).log(anyString());
    }

    @Test
    void cancelOrder_alreadyCancelled_shouldNotDelete() {
        Order order = new Order(1L, "Widget", OrderStatus.CANCELLED);
        when(orderRepo.findById(1L)).thenReturn(Optional.of(order));

        orderService.cancelOrder(1L);

        verify(orderRepo, never()).delete(any()); // must NOT be called
        verify(eventPublisher, never()).publish(any());
    }
}
```

The first test verifies that cancelling an active order triggers a delete and publishes an event. The second test verifies that cancelling an already-cancelled order does nothing—using `never()` to assert methods were not called.

```java
@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class ReportServiceTest {

    @Mock private ReportRepository reportRepo;
    @Mock private EmailService emailService;

    @InjectMocks private ReportService reportService;

    @Test
    void generateReport_shouldSaveAndEmail() {
        // any() matches any argument of the inferred type
        when(reportRepo.save(any(Report.class))).thenAnswer(inv -> {
            Report r = inv.getArgument(0);
            r.setId(1L);
            return r;
        });

        reportService.generateAndSend("Sales", "manager@co.com");

        // eq() for exact match, anyString() for flexible match
        verify(emailService).send(
                eq("manager@co.com"),       // exact email
                anyString(),                // any subject line
                argThat(body -> body.contains("Sales")) // body must mention "Sales"
        );
    }

    @Test
    void findReports_byDateRange() {
        when(reportRepo.findByDateBetween(any(LocalDate.class), any(LocalDate.class)))
                .thenReturn(List.of(new Report("Q1")));

        List<Report> reports = reportService.getReportsForQuarter(1, 2025);

        assertThat(reports).hasSize(1);
        verify(reportRepo).findByDateBetween(
                eq(LocalDate.of(2025, 1, 1)),
                eq(LocalDate.of(2025, 3, 31))
        );
    }
}
```

The first test uses `argThat()` with a lambda predicate to verify that the email body contains specific content. The second test mixes `eq()` matchers to verify exact date arguments.

`argThat()` accepts a lambda predicate, giving you full control over matching logic. This is powerful for complex objects:

```java
verify(orderRepo).save(argThat(order ->
        order.getStatus() == OrderStatus.CONFIRMED &&
        order.getTotal().compareTo(BigDecimal.ZERO) > 0 &&
        order.getItems().size() >= 1
));
```

If the predicate fails, Mockito shows the expected matcher and the actual arguments, making debugging straightforward.

For reusable matchers, implement the `ArgumentMatcher<T>` interface:

```java
class OrderWithStatus implements ArgumentMatcher<Order> {
    private final OrderStatus expected;

    OrderWithStatus(OrderStatus expected) { this.expected = expected; }

    @Override
    public boolean matches(Order order) {
        return order != null && order.getStatus() == expected;
    }
}

// Usage
verify(orderRepo).save(argThat(new OrderWithStatus(OrderStatus.CONFIRMED)));
```

Custom matcher classes are worth creating when the same predicate is used across multiple test classes.

```java
@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class OrderServiceTest {

    @Mock private OrderRepository orderRepo;
    @Mock private EventPublisher eventPublisher;

    @Captor // shorthand for ArgumentCaptor.forClass()
    private ArgumentCaptor<OrderEvent> eventCaptor;

    @InjectMocks private OrderService orderService;

    @Test
    void placeOrder_shouldPublishEventWithCorrectDetails() {
        when(orderRepo.save(any())).thenAnswer(inv -> {
            Order o = inv.getArgument(0);
            o.setId(7L);
            return o;
        });

        orderService.placeOrder("Widget", 3, BigDecimal.valueOf(9.99));

        // Capture the event that was published
        verify(eventPublisher).publish(eventCaptor.capture());

        // Now inspect it with full AssertJ power
        OrderEvent event = eventCaptor.getValue();
        assertThat(event.getOrderId()).isEqualTo(7L);
        assertThat(event.getProductName()).isEqualTo("Widget");
        assertThat(event.getQuantity()).isEqualTo(3);
        assertThat(event.getTotal()).isEqualByComparingTo("29.97");
        assertThat(event.getTimestamp()).isNotNull();
    }
}
```

The `OrderEvent` is created inside `placeOrder()`—we never see it from the test. But `ArgumentCaptor` intercepts it when `eventPublisher.publish()` is called, letting us assert on every field.

When a mock method is called multiple times, `ArgumentCaptor` captures all invocations. Use `getAllValues()` to inspect them:

```java
@Test
void batchProcess_shouldSaveAllOrders() {
    ArgumentCaptor<Order> captor = ArgumentCaptor.forClass(Order.class);

    orderService.batchProcess(List.of("Widget", "Gadget", "Doohickey"));

    verify(orderRepo, times(3)).save(captor.capture());

    List<Order> savedOrders = captor.getAllValues();
    assertThat(savedOrders).hasSize(3);
    assertThat(savedOrders).extracting(Order::getProductName)
            .containsExactly("Widget", "Gadget", "Doohickey");
}
```

Each `capture()` call records one invocation. `getAllValues()` returns them in order. This is invaluable for testing batch operations, retry logic, or event streams where the same mock is called repeatedly with different arguments.

```java
@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class PaymentServiceTest {

    @Mock private PaymentGateway gateway;
    @Mock private TransactionRepository transactionRepo;

    @InjectMocks private PaymentService paymentService;

    @Test
    void charge_whenGatewayFails_shouldThrowPaymentException() {
        when(gateway.charge(anyString(), any(BigDecimal.class)))
                .thenThrow(new GatewayTimeoutException("Connection timed out"));

        assertThatThrownBy(() -> paymentService.processPayment("card-1", BigDecimal.TEN))
                .isInstanceOf(PaymentProcessingException.class)
                .hasMessageContaining("timed out")
                .hasCauseInstanceOf(GatewayTimeoutException.class);
    }

    @Test
    void charge_whenGatewayFails_shouldNotSaveTransaction() {
        when(gateway.charge(anyString(), any(BigDecimal.class)))
                .thenThrow(new GatewayTimeoutException("timeout"));

        try {
            paymentService.processPayment("card-1", BigDecimal.TEN);
        } catch (PaymentProcessingException ignored) {}

        verify(transactionRepo, never()).save(any()); // no side effects on failure
    }

    @Test
    void charge_retryOnFirstFailure_shouldSucceedOnSecond() {
        when(gateway.charge(anyString(), any(BigDecimal.class)))
                .thenThrow(new GatewayTimeoutException("first attempt"))
                .thenReturn(new ChargeResult(true, "txn-99")); // second attempt succeeds

        PaymentResult result = paymentService.processWithRetry("card-1", BigDecimal.TEN);

        assertThat(result.isSuccessful()).isTrue();
        verify(gateway, times(2)).charge(anyString(), any(BigDecimal.class));
    }
}
```

Three patterns: (1) verify the exception is wrapped correctly, (2) verify no side effects on failure, (3) chain `thenThrow().thenReturn()` to test retry logic.

The standard `when(mock.method())` syntax doesn't work for void methods because there's nothing to return. Use the `doThrow()` pattern instead:

```java
@Test
void deleteUser_whenRepoFails_shouldHandleGracefully() {
    doThrow(new DataAccessException("DB connection lost") {})
            .when(userRepo).deleteById(anyLong());

    assertThatThrownBy(() -> userService.removeUser(1L))
            .isInstanceOf(ServiceException.class);
}
```

The `doThrow().when()` pattern reverses the order: you specify the behavior first, then the mock and method. This family of methods includes:
- `doThrow(exception).when(mock).method()` — throw on void method
- `doNothing().when(mock).method()` — explicitly do nothing (useful for overriding previous stubs)
- `doAnswer(invocation -> ...).when(mock).method()` — custom behavior for void methods

Use this pattern any time you need to stub a void method, not just for exceptions.

Mockito enforces Java's checked exception rules. If a method's signature doesn't declare a checked exception, `thenThrow()` with a checked exception will fail at **test compile time** (or with a Mockito runtime error).

```java
// Method signature: Optional<User> findById(Long id);
// findById does NOT declare IOException

// WRONG — Mockito rejects this at runtime
when(repo.findById(1L)).thenThrow(new IOException("disk error"));

// CORRECT — use an unchecked exception
when(repo.findById(1L)).thenThrow(new RuntimeException("disk error"));

// CORRECT — use a domain-specific unchecked exception
when(repo.findById(1L)).thenThrow(new DataAccessException("disk error") {});
```

This is a common source of confusion. If you need to simulate a checked exception and the method doesn't declare it, either use an unchecked exception that wraps it, or reconsider whether the method should declare the checked exception in its signature.

For methods that DO declare checked exceptions (like `void sendEmail() throws MessagingException`), `thenThrow(new MessagingException())` works perfectly.

