Key differences from JUnit 4: no `public` required on test classes/methods, `@BeforeEach` replaces `@Before`, `@DisplayName` provides readable test output, and `@Nested` groups related tests inside inner classes.

```java
class DatabaseTest {

    private static Connection connection;

    @BeforeAll // runs ONCE before all tests in this class
    static void initDatabase() {
        connection = DriverManager.getConnection("jdbc:h2:mem:test");
        // create tables, seed reference data
    }

    @AfterAll // runs ONCE after all tests complete
    static void closeDatabase() {
        connection.close();
    }

    @BeforeEach // runs before EACH test method
    void cleanTables() {
        // truncate tables to ensure test isolation
    }

    @AfterEach // runs after EACH test method
    void logResult(TestInfo testInfo) {
        System.out.println("Completed: " + testInfo.getDisplayName());
    }

    @Test
    @DisplayName("Insert user should return generated ID")
    void insertUser() {
        // test code here
    }

    @Test
    @Disabled("Blocked by issue #42")
    void updateUser() {
        // skipped until issue is resolved
    }
}
```

Execution order: `@BeforeAll` → (`@BeforeEach` → `@Test` → `@AfterEach`) × N tests → `@AfterAll`. Note that `@BeforeAll` and `@AfterAll` are `static` by default (unless using `@TestInstance(PER_CLASS)`).

`@Tag` lets you categorize tests for selective execution:

```java
@Tag("fast")
@Test
void quickCalculation() { ... }

@Tag("slow")
@Test
void complexIntegration() { ... }
```

In Maven: `<groups>fast</groups>` runs only fast tests. In Gradle: `useJUnitPlatform { includeTags 'fast' }`.

`@TestMethodOrder` controls execution order:

```java
@TestMethodOrder(MethodOrderer.OrderAnnotation.class)
class OrderedTest {
    @Test @Order(1) void createUser() { ... }
    @Test @Order(2) void updateUser() { ... }
    @Test @Order(3) void deleteUser() { ... }
}
```

Use ordered tests sparingly—independent tests are more maintainable. But for API integration tests that follow a workflow, ordering can make the test read like a specification.

`@RepeatedTest(10)` runs a test multiple times, useful for catching race conditions or flaky behavior in concurrent code.

JUnit 5 provides annotations to run tests only under specific conditions:

```java
@Test
@EnabledOnOs(OS.LINUX)
void linuxSpecificTest() { ... }

@Test
@EnabledForJreRange(min = JRE.JAVA_17)
void java17FeatureTest() { ... }

@Test
@EnabledIfEnvironmentVariable(named = "CI", matches = "true")
void ciOnlyTest() { ... }

@Test
@EnabledIfSystemProperty(named = "os.arch", matches = "aarch64")
void armSpecificTest() { ... }
```

These annotations are invaluable for tests that depend on the environment: OS-specific file paths, JDK version features, CI-only smoke tests, or architecture-specific optimizations. They're cleaner than putting `Assumptions.assumeTrue()` at the top of every test.

The `@Disabled` annotation with a reason is the simplest conditional—it always skips: `@Disabled("Waiting for API v2 release")`.

```java
class AssertionExamples {

    @Test
    void junitAssertions() {
        // Basic equality
        assertEquals(4, calculator.add(2, 2));
        assertEquals(3.14, calculator.pi(), 0.01); // delta for floating point

        // Boolean checks
        assertTrue(user.isActive());
        assertFalse(user.isLocked());

        // Null checks
        assertNotNull(user.getId());
        assertNull(user.getDeletedAt());

        // Exception assertion
        assertThrows(IllegalArgumentException.class,
                () -> calculator.divide(1, 0));

        // Grouped assertions — all evaluated even if some fail
        assertAll("user properties",
                () -> assertEquals("Alice", user.getName()),
                () -> assertEquals("alice@mail.com", user.getEmail()),
                () -> assertTrue(user.isActive())
        );
    }

    @Test
    void assertJExamples() {
        // Fluent, readable API
        assertThat(calculator.add(2, 2)).isEqualTo(4);
        assertThat(user.getName()).isEqualTo("Alice");
        assertThat(user.isActive()).isTrue();
        assertThat(user.getId()).isNotNull();

        // String assertions
        assertThat(user.getEmail())
                .contains("@")
                .endsWith(".com")
                .doesNotContain("spam");

        // Collection assertions
        assertThat(users)
                .hasSize(3)
                .extracting(User::getName)
                .containsExactly("Alice", "Bob", "Charlie");

        // Exception assertions
        assertThatThrownBy(() -> calculator.divide(1, 0))
                .isInstanceOf(ArithmeticException.class)
                .hasMessageContaining("zero");
    }
}
```

AssertJ's fluent API reads more naturally: `assertThat(actual).isEqualTo(expected)` vs `assertEquals(expected, actual)`. The parameter order in JUnit's `assertEquals` (expected first, actual second) is a common source of confusion.

One of JUnit 5's most useful additions is `assertAll()`. Without it, a test with multiple assertions stops at the first failure:

```java
// Without assertAll — stops at first failure
assertEquals("Alice", user.getName());     // FAILS → test stops here
assertEquals("alice@mail.com", user.getEmail()); // never evaluated
assertTrue(user.isActive());                     // never evaluated
```

With `assertAll()`, all assertions are evaluated and all failures are reported:

```java
assertAll("user properties",
    () -> assertEquals("Alice", user.getName()),       // FAILS
    () -> assertEquals("alice@mail.com", user.getEmail()), // also evaluated
    () -> assertTrue(user.isActive())                      // also evaluated
);
```

This gives you a **complete picture** of what's wrong in a single test run, rather than fixing failures one by one. It's especially valuable when asserting on multiple fields of a returned object.

AssertJ provides equivalent behavior with `SoftAssertions`:

```java
SoftAssertions.assertSoftly(softly -> {
    softly.assertThat(user.getName()).isEqualTo("Alice");
    softly.assertThat(user.getEmail()).endsWith(".com");
});
```

