Each row in the data source produces a separate test execution. The IDE shows each case individually, making it easy to identify which input caused a failure.

**Cycle 1 — Red:** write a test for an empty stack.
```java
@Test
void newStack_shouldBeEmpty() {
    MyStack<Integer> stack = new MyStack<>();
    assertThat(stack.isEmpty()).isTrue();
    assertThat(stack.size()).isEqualTo(0);
}
```

**Cycle 1 — Green:** write minimum code.
```java
public class MyStack<T> {
    public boolean isEmpty() { return true; }
    public int size() { return 0; }
}
```

**Cycle 2 — Red:** test pushing an element.
```java
@Test
void push_shouldIncreaseSize() {
    MyStack<Integer> stack = new MyStack<>();
    stack.push(42);
    assertThat(stack.isEmpty()).isFalse();
    assertThat(stack.size()).isEqualTo(1);
}
```

**Cycle 2 — Green:** add state management.
```java
public class MyStack<T> {
    private final List<T> elements = new ArrayList<>();

    public void push(T item) { elements.add(item); }
    public boolean isEmpty() { return elements.isEmpty(); }
    public int size() { return elements.size(); }
}
```

**Cycle 3 — Red:** test pop.
```java
@Test
void pop_shouldReturnLastPushed() {
    MyStack<Integer> stack = new MyStack<>();
    stack.push(1);
    stack.push(2);
    assertThat(stack.pop()).isEqualTo(2);
    assertThat(stack.size()).isEqualTo(1);
}
```

Each cycle adds one behavior with one test. The code evolves incrementally with full test coverage guaranteed.
